{
 "category": "40-option-implied-derivatives",
 "cards": [
  {
   "id": 340,
   "method": "Black-Scholes and Bachelier pricing, Greeks and implied-volatility inversion",
   "wrapper_file": "c40_option_implied_derivatives/bs_pricing_iv.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "opt_price",
    "opt_implied_volatility"
   ],
   "when": "pricing a vanilla option, computing its sensitivities, or backing out the volatility the market price implies",
   "when_not": "the option is path dependent or American with early exercise that matters (-> a lattice or Monte Carlo); the underlying can go negative and the model is lognormal",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "Bachelier (normal) model",
     "criterion": "the underlying can be negative, as with spreads and some rates"
    },
    {
     "alt": "binomial or trinomial lattice",
     "criterion": "early exercise matters"
    },
    {
     "alt": "stochastic volatility",
     "criterion": "the smile must be fitted, not assumed away"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "price: the model value",
    "delta, gamma, vega, theta, rho: the Greeks",
    "implied_volatility: the inverted volatility, None where no solution exists",
    "moneyness, time_to_expiry: the inputs, echoed for auditability"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "implied volatility DOES NOT EXIST when the price violates the no-arbitrage bounds; returning None is correct and a returned number in that case is a solver artefact",
    "deep in- and out-of-the-money inversions are numerically ill-conditioned because vega goes to zero -- small price errors become large volatility errors",
    "Black-Scholes and Bachelier volatilities are in different units and are not comparable"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Black & Scholes 1973 (Journal of Political Economy 81:637-654)",
    "Bachelier 1900, Theorie de la speculation",
    "Jaeckel 2015, Let's be rational (Wilmott 2015:40)",
    "vollib"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 341,
   "method": "Implied-volatility surface fitting with SVI and SABR, and arbitrage checks",
   "wrapper_file": "c40_option_implied_derivatives/iv_surface.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "opt_fit_surface",
    "opt_surface_evaluate",
    "opt_arbitrage_check"
   ],
   "when": "building a smooth, arbitrage-free volatility surface from discrete quoted options, so any strike and maturity can be priced consistently",
   "when_not": "you only need one option's implied volatility; the quote set is too sparse for a surface to be identified",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "SVI",
     "criterion": "a slice-wise parameterisation with known arbitrage conditions"
    },
    {
     "alt": "SABR",
     "criterion": "you want a dynamic model consistent with smile movement"
    },
    {
     "alt": "interpolation",
     "criterion": "you only need to fill gaps and do not need a model"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "params: the fitted surface parameters per slice",
    "fitted_iv: implied volatility on the requested grid",
    "butterfly_arbitrage, calendar_arbitrage: violation flags and where they occur",
    "rmse: fit quality against the quotes"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "heatmap",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "a surface that fits well can still admit ARBITRAGE; the butterfly and calendar checks are not diagnostics of fit, they are constraints on validity",
    "SVI in raw parameterisation has parameter combinations that guarantee butterfly arbitrage -- fitting without the constraint routinely lands there",
    "extrapolating the surface beyond the quoted strike range is unconstrained by data and is where most pricing errors originate"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Gatheral & Jacquier 2014 (Quantitative Finance 14:59-71)",
    "Hagan, Kumar, Lesniewski & Woodward 2002, Managing smile risk (Wilmott 1:84)",
    "Gatheral 2006, The Volatility Surface",
    "QuantLib"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 342,
   "method": "Risk-neutral density extraction by Breeden-Litzenberger",
   "wrapper_file": "c40_option_implied_derivatives/risk_neutral_density.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "opt_risk_neutral_density"
   ],
   "when": "recovering the market's implied distribution of the underlying from a cross-section of option prices -- the density behind every option-implied uncertainty measure",
   "when_not": "the strike grid is too coarse or too noisy for a second derivative to be estimated; you need the physical rather than risk-neutral measure",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "parametric density fitting",
     "criterion": "you want a smooth family rather than a nonparametric density"
    },
    {
     "alt": "Breeden-Litzenberger on a fitted surface",
     "criterion": "the standard route: smooth first, differentiate second"
    },
    {
     "alt": "mixture of lognormals",
     "criterion": "you want an interpretable multi-modal fit"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "density: the risk-neutral density on the strike grid",
    "cdf: the implied distribution function",
    "quantiles: implied quantiles, which are the usable summary",
    "negative_mass: where the density came out negative, which signals arbitrage or over-fitting"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the density is a SECOND DERIVATIVE of price in strike, so it amplifies quote noise enormously -- differentiating raw quotes is never acceptable",
    "negative density values mean the fitted price function is not convex, i.e. butterfly arbitrage; they are a validity failure, not a rounding issue",
    "the recovered density is RISK-NEUTRAL and is not the market's physical forecast; the difference is the risk premium"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Breeden & Litzenberger 1978 (Journal of Business 51:621-651)",
    "Ait-Sahalia & Lo 1998 (Journal of Finance 53:499)",
    "Figlewski 2010, Estimating the implied risk-neutral density (Volatility and Time Series Econometrics)"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 343,
   "method": "Model-free implied variance and variance swaps",
   "wrapper_file": "c40_option_implied_derivatives/model_free_variance.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "opt_model_free_variance",
    "opt_variance_risk_premium"
   ],
   "when": "computing the VIX-style expected variance from an option strip without assuming any pricing model",
   "when_not": "the strike range is too narrow for the integral to converge; you want a model-based volatility instead",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "Black-Scholes at-the-money implied volatility",
     "criterion": "a single quick proxy"
    },
    {
     "alt": "model-free implied variance",
     "criterion": "the model-free expectation under the risk-neutral measure"
    },
    {
     "alt": "realised variance",
     "criterion": "the physical rather than risk-neutral quantity"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "implied_variance: the model-free variance for the maturity",
    "implied_volatility: its annualised square root",
    "truncation_error: the contribution the missing strike wings would have made",
    "variance_risk_premium: implied minus realised, where realised is supplied"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the integral requires strikes over the WHOLE positive axis; a finite strike range truncates it and systematically understates variance, especially in stressed markets",
    "the VIX construction interpolates two maturities to a constant horizon; comparing a raw single-maturity figure with VIX is not like for like",
    "implied variance is risk-neutral and exceeds expected realised variance by the variance risk premium -- it is not a forecast"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Britten-Jones & Neuberger 2000 (Journal of Finance 55:839-866)",
    "Carr & Wu 2009 (Review of Financial Studies 22:1311)",
    "CBOE 2019, VIX White Paper"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 344,
   "method": "Risk-neutral moments as uncertainty proxies",
   "wrapper_file": "c40_option_implied_derivatives/risk_neutral_moments.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "opt_risk_neutral_moments"
   ],
   "when": "summarising the option-implied distribution in variance, skewness and kurtosis -- the standard market-based measures of uncertainty and crash fear",
   "when_not": "the option cross-section is too thin for higher moments, which need the wings most of all",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "implied variance alone",
     "criterion": "only the second moment is needed"
    },
    {
     "alt": "risk-neutral skewness",
     "criterion": "asymmetry and crash risk are the question"
    },
    {
     "alt": "full density extraction",
     "criterion": "you want the whole distribution rather than moments"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "variance, skewness, kurtosis: the risk-neutral moments",
    "skew_measure: the strike-based skew slope, an alternative summary",
    "wing_contribution: how much of each moment comes from the extrapolated wings"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "higher moments are dominated by the WINGS, which is exactly where quotes are sparsest and extrapolation is least reliable -- report the wing contribution",
    "risk-neutral skewness is not physical skewness; it embeds the price of crash risk",
    "the moments require a strike grid dense enough for numerical integration; a handful of quotes gives numbers without content"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Bakshi, Kapadia & Madan 2003 (Review of Financial Studies 16:101-143)",
    "Carr & Madan 2001, Optimal positioning in derivative securities (Quantitative Finance 1:19)"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 345,
   "method": "Term structure of option-implied uncertainty and density fan charts",
   "wrapper_file": "c40_option_implied_derivatives/implied_term_structure.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "opt_implied_term_structure",
    "opt_density_fan"
   ],
   "when": "reading how market uncertainty is priced across horizons, and turning the implied densities into a fan chart",
   "when_not": "options exist at one maturity only; you want a model-based rather than market-implied forecast",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "implied variance term structure",
     "criterion": "the horizon shape of uncertainty"
    },
    {
     "alt": "forward variance",
     "criterion": "the variance priced between two future dates"
    },
    {
     "alt": "density fan chart",
     "criterion": "you want quantile bands rather than a single number"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "term_structure: implied variance or volatility per maturity",
    "forward_variance: variance between successive maturities",
    "fan: implied quantile bands per horizon",
    "slope, curvature: summary shape statistics"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "multi-line",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "FORWARD variance computed from two maturities can come out negative when the term structure is non-monotonic and the quotes are noisy -- that is a data problem surfacing, not a calculation error",
    "the fan chart is risk-neutral and is systematically wider than a physical forecast interval",
    "maturities must be aligned to a common convention before differencing; mixing calendar and trading-day conventions distorts the whole curve"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Carr & Wu 2009 (Review of Financial Studies 22:1311-1341)",
    "Andersen, Bondarenko & Gonzalez-Perez 2015 (Review of Financial Studies 28:3437)",
    "QuantLib"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  },
  {
   "id": 346,
   "method": "Stochastic-volatility models: Heston, log-normal SV and rough volatility, with calibration",
   "wrapper_file": "c40_option_implied_derivatives/stochastic_volatility_models.py",
   "tool_fns": [
    "opt_sv_calibrate",
    "opt_sv_price",
    "opt_sv_simulate"
   ],
   "when": "the smile and its dynamics matter, so volatility must be a process rather than a number",
   "when_not": "a single vanilla price is all you need (-> Black-Scholes); the option data is too sparse to identify several parameters",
   "alternatives": [
    {
     "alt": "Heston",
     "criterion": "closed-form characteristic function and fast calibration"
    },
    {
     "alt": "log-normal stochastic volatility",
     "criterion": "you want volatility to stay positive by construction"
    },
    {
     "alt": "rough volatility",
     "criterion": "the short-maturity smile is steeper than any classical model can fit"
    }
   ],
   "output_key_fields": [
    "params: the calibrated parameters",
    "fit_rmse: calibration error against the quoted surface",
    "feller_satisfied: whether the Heston variance can reach zero",
    "implied_surface: the model surface, for comparison with the market"
   ],
   "chart_kind": "table",
   "interpretation_traps": [
    "the Heston FELLER CONDITION is routinely violated by calibrated parameters, which lets variance hit zero and makes simulation schemes fail silently",
    "calibration is a non-convex problem with many local optima; a good fit from one starting point is not the global fit",
    "rough volatility fits the short end far better and costs a great deal more to simulate; the trade-off is real"
   ],
   "sources": [
    "Heston 1993 (Review of Financial Studies 6:327-343)",
    "Gatheral, Jaisson & Rosenbaum 2018 (Quantitative Finance 18:933)",
    "Bergomi 2016, Stochastic Volatility Modeling",
    "stochvolmodels"
   ],
   "precondition_tools": null,
   "precondition_gates": null,
   "validation_notes": null
  }
 ],
